 Mental disorders are a prevalent problem of our world, 10.7% of people worldwide live with a mental health disorder111https://ourworldindata.org/mental-health. There are various mental illnesses such as depression, anxiety, bipolar disorder, eating disorders or addictions (including alcohol, drugs or gambling). For many people living with these problems, it is hard to access  professional help and the problem may worsen over time. There is a need to develop methods to automatically detect the risk of mental health problems and to provide support to those in need.

The eRisk workshop\footnote{\url{https://erisk2021.upf.edu/}} is an international initiative that aims to bring together researchers from various disciplines (e.g. psychology, psychiatry, medicine, computer science, engineering, social science) to develop methods for early risk detection of mental health disorders using social media data. It focuses on three major tasks: (1) early risk detection of depression, (2) early risk detection of self-harm, (3) early detection of pathological gambling and (4) early risk detection of eating disorders. In this paper, we present the contributions of the BLUE team\footnote{\url{https://github.com/BlueAgent/}} to the first three tasks.

The paper is structured as follows: In Section \ref{sec:related}, we present related work on the three tasks. In Section \ref{sec:data}, we describe the data that we used for our experiments. Section \ref{sec:method} contains the description of our methods. Section \ref{sec:experiments} contains the results of our experiments. Finally, in Section \ref{sec:conclusion} we present our conclusions.
Related Work
\label{sec:related}

\subsection{Depression}
Depression is a serious medical illness that negatively affects how you feel, the way you think and how you act. It can lead to a variety of emotional and physical problems and can decrease a person's ability to function in everyday life. The World Health Organization (WHO) estimates that more than 264 million people suffer from depression. The early risk detection of depression is a very active area of research, as evidenced by the number of related papers that are published each year \cite{arxivID-1710.05429-0, arxivID-2211.07717-1, arxivID-2204.10841-2, arxivID-2106.10485-3, arxivID-2306.17564-4, arxivID-2406.06774-5, arxivID-2406.03138-6, arxivID-2409.14312-7, arxivID-2305.06447-8}. The eRisk workshop has also organized several tasks on the early risk detection of depression \cite{arxivID-1302.1178-9, arxivID-1203.0518-10, arxivID-1201.0274-11}. The best method for the 2020 edition of the task was proposed by \cite{arxivID-1804.07000-12} and obtained an F1 score of 0.72.

\subsection{Self-harm}
Self-harm is the act of hurting oneself on purpose, such as cutting oneself with a knife, banging one's head against a wall or taking an overdose of pills. Self-harm is a common problem among adolescents and young adults, with an estimated 19.1\% of young people having engaged in self-harm at some point \cite{arxivID-2305.15746-13}. The early risk detection of self-harm is a very active area of research \cite{arxivID-2207.07014-14, arxivID-1206.5520-15, arxivID-1804.07253-16, arxivID-2104.00174-17, arxivID-1702.06875-18, arxivID-2206.08673-19, arxivID-2012.09123-20}

