 We now live in a world with various modalities(voice, vision, odors, etc. ), among which vision and language are two critical ones. In academia, there exist large amounts  of multimodal data, such as images with captions, videos with subtitles, audio with captions, etc. In industry, multimodal data are also ubiquitous, such as videos, images, and audio on YouTube, Instagram, and TikTok. Multimodal learning aims to learn the representation of multimodal data and explore the relationship between different modalities. It is an essential step toward artificial general intelligence(AGI). \cite{arxivID-2209.03430-0}.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centerline{\includegraphics[width=0.4\textwidth]{figure/figure1.png}}
\caption{The six core challenges in multimodal machine learning.}
\label{fig}
\end{figure}

Multimodal learning can be classified into three categories: multi-view learning, multi-modal learning, and multi-task learning. Multi-view learning aims to learn from multiple views of the same data, such as videos, images, and text. Multi-modal learning aims to learn from multiple modalities of the same data, such as images and text. Multi-task learning aims to learn from multiple tasks of the same data, such as image classification and image captioning. In this paper, we focus on multi-modal learning. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:1}, multi-modal learning is divided into two subcategories: uni-modal learning and cross-modal learning. Uni-modal learning refers to the task that only uses one modality, such as image classification, image captioning, and visual question answering. Cross-modal learning refers to the task that uses multiple modalities, such as visual grounding and visual dialog.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centerline{\includegraphics[width=0.4\textwidth]{figure/figure2.png}}
\caption{The two subcategories of multi-modal learning.}
\label{fig:1}
\end{figure}

Uni-modal learning has been well-studied in the past few years. For example, image classification is a classical task in computer vision, and the performance of image classification has been significantly improved by using deep learning techniques \cite{arxivID-2105.04895-1, arxivID-2001.06268-2, arxivID-2001.06268-3}. Image captioning is a classical task in natural language processing, and the performance of image captioning has been significantly improved by using deep learning techniques \cite{arxivID-2201.12944-4, arxivID-1810.04020-5}. Visual question answering is a classical task in multimodal learning, and the performance of visual question answering has been significantly improved by using deep learning techniques \cite{arxivID-1610.01465-6, arxivID-1610.01465-7}. However, cross-modal learning has not been well-studied in the past few years. For example, visual grounding is a classical task in computer vision, and the performance of visual grounding has not been significantly improved by using deep learning techniques \cite{arxivID-2105.04281-8}. Visual dialog is a classical task in natural language processing, and the performance of visual dialog has not been significantly improved by using deep learning techniques \cite{arxivID-1802.03803-9}. Therefore, cross-modal learning is a challenging and important task in multimodal learning.

Cross-modal learning is a challenging task in multimodal learning, and it is also an important task in multimodal learning. It has many applications in real life, such as visual grounding, visual dialog, etc. However, cross-modal learning has not been well-studied in the past few years. For example, visual grounding is a classical task in computer vision, and the performance of visual grounding has not been significantly improved by using deep learning techniques \cite{arxivID-2104.08541-10}

 In human communication, speakers often adjust their language production by taking into consideration listeners personality, background knowledge, perceptual or physical capabilities etc #ref3#. Recent years have seen an increasing amount of work that explores pragmatic reasoning based on Rational Speech Act (RSA)  #ref1# #ref10# #ref11# #ref36# #ref4#, multi-agent emergent communication framework #ref22# #ref21#, and Theory of Mind in communication #ref2# #ref38#. However, except for  #ref38#, most previous works assume that the listeners and the speakers  share the same language, and the speaker only needs to reason about the listeners' knowledge background. In this work, we extend the RSA framework and consider a more realistic setting that the speaker and the listener may have different language abilities, e.g. the speaker is able to produce longer and more complex sentences, while the listener is only able to understand shorter and simpler sentences. We aim to learn the speaker's strategy that is able to adjust its speech accordingly, to better match the listener's language ability.

We propose the Pragmatic Rational Speaker (PRS), a framework extending the Rational Speech Act (RSA) \cite{arxivID-2105.09867-0}. The PRS attempts to learn the speaker-listener disparity and adjust the speech accordingly, by adding a light-weighted disparity adjustment layer into working memory on top of speakers long-term memory system. By fixing the long-term memory, the PRS only needs to update its working memory to learn and adapt to different types of listeners. To validate our framework, we create a dataset that simulates different types of speaker-listener disparities in the context of referential games. Our empirical results demonstrate that the PRS is able to shift its output towards the language that listeners are able to understand, significantly improve the collaborative task outcome.

The main contributions of this paper are:
\begin{itemize}
\item We propose a new framework for pragmatic reasoning, Pragmatic Rational Speaker (PRS), that learns to adjust its speech production according to the disparity between the speaker and the listener.
\item We create a referential game dataset that simulates different types of speaker-listener disparities. We believe that the dataset could be used as a benchmark for evaluating pragmatic reasoning in future research.
\item We show that the PRS is able to learn to adjust its speech production to match the listener's ability, and improve the collaborative task outcome.
\end{itemize}
